candidate.formal_statement_latex:
Assume that quantum-secure one-way functions exist. Then there exist polynomially bounded parameters $h,n,n',t,t'$ (functions of $\eta$) and an $(h,n,n',t,t')$-encryption scheme $\Pi=(\mathsf{KGen},\mathsf{Enc},\mathsf{Dec})$ such that both of the following hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\Pi$ is $\mathrm{learn}(*,CL)$-$\mathrm{chall}(1,ER,2\mathrm{ct})$-IND-CPA-secure, i.e.\ every QPT adversary that makes polynomially many classical learning queries and \emph{exactly one} $(ER,2\mathrm{ct})$-challenge query wins the game with probability at most $\tfrac12+\varepsilon(\eta)$ for some negligible $\varepsilon$.
\item $\Pi$ is \emph{not} $\mathrm{learn}(*,EM)$-$\mathrm{chall}(*,CL,1\mathrm{ct})$-IND-CPA-secure: there exist a QPT adversary $\mathcal{A}$ making polynomially many $EM$-learning queries and polynomially many $(CL,1\mathrm{ct})$-challenge queries, and a polynomial $p$, such that $\mathcal{A}$ wins that game with probability at least $\tfrac12+1/p(\eta)$ for infinitely many $\eta$.
\end{enumerate}

candidate.definitions_latex:
\begin{definition}[Query types]
Fix an $(h,n,n',t,t')$-encryption scheme $\Pi$, a key $k\in\{0,1\}^{h}$ and a bit $b\in\{0,1\}$. In each query below the randomness values $r,r_0,r_1$ are sampled freshly and uniformly from $\{0,1\}^{t}$; a single superposition query uses one value of $r$ for all messages in the superposition.
\begin{itemize}
\item A \emph{$CL$-learning query}: the adversary sends a classical $m\in\{0,1\}^{n}$ and receives $\mathsf{Enc}_k(m;r)$.
\item An \emph{$EM$-learning query}: the adversary hands over $Q_{in}$ ($n$ qubits); the challenger prepares $Q_{out}$ in state $|0\rangle^{\otimes n'}$, applies $U_{\mathsf{Enc}_k(\cdot\,;r)}$ to $(Q_{in},Q_{out})$, and returns both registers.
\item A \emph{$(CL,1\mathrm{ct})$-challenge query}: the adversary sends classical $m_0,m_1\in\{0,1\}^{n}$ and receives the classical ciphertext $\mathsf{Enc}_k(m_b;r)$.
\item An \emph{$(ER,2\mathrm{ct})$-challenge query}: the adversary hands over registers $Q_{in0},Q_{in1}$ (each $n$ qubits); the challenger applies the isometry $|m_0\rangle|m_1\rangle\mapsto|\mathsf{Enc}_k(m_b;r_0)\rangle|\mathsf{Enc}_k(m_{\bar b};r_1)\rangle$ and returns the two resulting $n'$-qubit registers.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\mathfrak{l}$-$\mathfrak{c}$-IND-CPA]
Let $\mathfrak{l}$ name a learning-query type and $\mathfrak{c}$ a challenge-query type together with a permitted number of challenge queries, $1$ or $*$ (polynomially many). In the game the challenger samples $k\leftarrow\mathsf{KGen}()$ and a uniform $b\in\{0,1\}$; a QPT adversary $\mathcal{A}$ makes polynomially many learning queries of type $\mathfrak{l}$ and the permitted number of challenge queries, in any interleaved order (learning queries may be made before and after a challenge query), all answered with this same $k$ and this same $b$; finally $\mathcal{A}$ outputs a bit $b'$ and wins iff $b'=b$. We write $\mathrm{learn}(*,X)$-$\mathrm{chall}(\mathfrak{c}_{\mathrm{nb}},Y,\mathrm{rt})$ for this game with $X$-learning queries and $\mathfrak{c}_{\mathrm{nb}}\in\{1,*\}$ challenge queries of type $(Y,\mathrm{rt})$, and call $\Pi$ secure for it iff every QPT adversary wins with probability at most $\tfrac12+\varepsilon(\eta)$ for some negligible $\varepsilon$.
\end{definition}
